<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency-Check suppressions.

  A suppression is a claim that a reported CVE does not apply to this project, and it is only
  honest if it says why. Every entry MUST carry:

    * the CVE it suppresses,
    * a dated note explaining why this project is not affected (wrong component matched, the
      vulnerable code path is unreachable here, fixed in the version we actually use, …),
    * where possible, an <until> date, so the claim expires and has to be re-argued rather than
      quietly outliving the reason it was made.

  An empty file is the honest starting state: nothing has been waived. If the scan fails, the first
  response is to upgrade the dependency, not to add an entry here. Suppressing a finding to make a
  build green is exactly the move ENGINEERING_STANDARDS §4 exists to prevent — the same rule the
  ArchUnit and coverage gates get.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    CVE-2023-5443 matched against THIS PROJECT'S OWN MODULES, not against a dependency.

    Added 2026-07-25, on the scan's first real run (the one after NVD_API_KEY was configured).
    Dependency-Check assigned every com.stoicera.einvoice artifact the CPE
    `cpe:2.3:a:e-invoice_project:e-invoice` purely because the groupId contains the word
    "einvoice", and then reported that unrelated product's CVE against all seven modules.

    This is the wrong-component case the header allows, and it is unambiguous: CVE-2023-5443 is a
    vulnerability in a different piece of software that this repository neither contains nor
    depends on. The suppression is scoped by packageUrl to our own groupId, so it can never mask a
    finding in a third-party dependency — the only thing the scan exists to find.

    No <until>: this claim does not decay, because it is about mistaken identity rather than about
    a version we might later upgrade past. Revisit only if the module coordinates change.
  -->
  <suppress>
    <notes><![CDATA[
      False positive: our own multi-module artifacts CPE-matched to the unrelated
      "e-invoice_project:e-invoice" product on a groupId substring. See the block comment above.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/com\.stoicera\.einvoice/.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2023-5443</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    CVE-2026-53914 (CVSS 9.8) against org.jetbrains.kotlin:kotlin-stdlib 2.3.21.

    Added 2026-07-27, on the M6 branch's first CI run — the same run that raised CVE-2026-39883
    against opentelemetry-semconv. That one was UPGRADED, not suppressed (root POM,
    opentelemetry-semconv.version), which is the policy this header states and the order these two
    were handled in.

    Why this one is different, and why it is a wrong-component match rather than an inconvenience:

      * The advisory reads, in full: "In JetBrains Kotlin before 2.4.20 code execution was possible
        via unsafe deserialization in THE BUILD CACHE METADATA." The Kotlin build cache is a feature
        of the Kotlin Gradle plugin and compiler — build-time tooling. `kotlin-stdlib` is the
        RUNTIME library and contains no build-cache code at all.
      * This project contains no Kotlin and compiles none. The jar arrives transitively, three
        levels down: spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry -> opentelemetry-exporter-otlp ->
        opentelemetry-exporter-sender-okhttp -> okhttp-jvm 5.3.2 -> kotlin-stdlib. It is OkHttp's
        runtime, shipped and never built against.
      * Dependency-Check matched it on `cpe:/a:jetbrains:kotlin` plus the version range. That CPE
        covers the whole Kotlin product, so every artifact of the distribution inherits every
        advisory about any part of it.

    Why not simply upgrade, which is what the header demands first: the fix version the advisory
    names is 2.4.20, and as of 2026-07-27 Maven Central publishes only 2.4.20-Beta2 — the latest
    STABLE is 2.4.10, which is still inside the vulnerable range. Pinning a beta of the Kotlin
    runtime underneath OkHttp, into a production container image, to dodge a build-tooling CVE this
    project cannot reach, would be a worse trade than a documented and expiring suppression. It
    would also override what OkHttp 5.3.2 was built and tested against.

    <until> is set deliberately and near: this claim SHOULD decay. When Kotlin 2.4.20 goes stable
    and OkHttp resolves at or above it, the finding disappears on its own and this entry must be
    deleted rather than renewed by reflex. If it expires while still needed, re-argue it — that is
    the point.
  -->
  <suppress until="2026-11-30Z">
    <notes><![CDATA[
      False positive for this artifact: CVE-2026-53914 is unsafe deserialization in the Kotlin BUILD
      CACHE METADATA (build tooling). kotlin-stdlib is the runtime library and carries no
      build-cache code; this project compiles no Kotlin and pulls the jar transitively via OkHttp,
      which the OTLP exporter uses. Fix version 2.4.20 exists only as a Beta on Maven Central as of
      2026-07-27, so an upgrade would mean shipping a beta runtime. See the block comment above.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.jetbrains\.kotlin/kotlin-stdlib@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-53914</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    CVE-2026-66299 (NVD CVSS 7.5, Apache severity: Low) against
    org.apache.tomcat.embed:tomcat-embed-core 11.0.24, Spring Boot 4.1.0's managed Tomcat.

    Added 2026-08-06, when the NVD published the CVE and turned the gate red on a repo whose code
    had not changed (the second such event that week, after the DOMPurify batch handled by
    upgrading springdoc — upgrade first, exactly as the header demands).

    Why this is a wrong-component match: the advisory, per Apache's own security page, is an
    unbounded buffer in the WEBSOCKET CHAT EXAMPLE — a page of tomcat.apache.org's distribution
    ("Users who followed the security guidance to remove the examples web application are not
    affected"). The examples webapp is part of the Tomcat DISTRIBUTION bundle; tomcat-embed-core
    is the embeddable servlet engine and does not contain or deploy the examples application, and
    a Spring Boot fat jar deploys no webapps at all. The vulnerable code cannot be present in this
    artifact, let alone reachable. Dependency-Check matched on `cpe:/a:apache:tomcat` + version
    range, which covers the whole product and so inherits an advisory about a part this jar does
    not carry.

    Why not simply upgrade, which is what the header demands first: the fix version is Tomcat
    11.0.25, and as of 2026-08-06 Maven Central does not publish tomcat-embed-core 11.0.25
    (directory 404), nor is there a Spring Boot 4.1.x patch managing it. When either appears,
    Dependabot's weekly run will surface the bump and this entry must be deleted rather than
    renewed by reflex.

    Scoped to the whole org.apache.tomcat.embed GROUP, not one artifact, learned the concrete way:
    the first version of this entry named tomcat-embed-core, and the very next scan raised the
    same CVE against tomcat-embed-websocket — every embed artifact carries the product-wide
    `cpe:/a:apache:tomcat` and so inherits every advisory about any part of the distribution. The
    argument is identical for all of them: no embed jar ships the examples webapp. Still pinned to
    this one CVE, so a genuine embed vulnerability is not maskable through this entry.

    <until> is set deliberately and near, for the same reason as the entry above: the claim should
    decay together with the release trains that make it moot.
  -->
  <suppress until="2026-10-31Z">
    <notes><![CDATA[
      False positive for these artifacts: CVE-2026-66299 is an unbounded buffer in the WebSocket
      chat EXAMPLE application of the Tomcat distribution. No org.apache.tomcat.embed artifact
      ships the examples webapp, and this Spring Boot application deploys no webapps. Fix version
      11.0.25 is not on Maven Central as of 2026-08-06 and no Boot 4.1.x patch manages it yet.
      See the block comment.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.tomcat\.embed/tomcat-embed-.*@.*$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-66299</cve>
  </suppress>

</suppressions>
